{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel were greatly afraid and cried to HaShem</b>, <b>and they said to Moses: Because there were no graves in Egypt</b> — it does not seem [correct] that people who cry out to HaShem to save them would kick against the salvation He performed for them and say that it would have been better for them had He not saved them. Rather, the correct interpretation is that there were different groups, and Scripture recounts everything that all of them did. It states that one group cried out to HaShem, and another group denied the prophet and did not acknowledge the salvation performed for them, and they said that it would have been better for them had He not saved them. Concerning this it is written: \"They rebelled at the sea, at the Sea of Suf\" (Psalms 106:7), and therefore Scripture repeats \"the children of Israel\" once again — <b>and the children of Israel cried to HaShem</b> — because the good among them cried out to HaShem, while the rest rebelled against His word. Therefore it says afterward (Exodus 14:31): \"And the people feared HaShem, and believed in HaShem and in Moses His servant.\" It does not say \"and Israel feared HaShem and believed,\" but it says \"the people,\" because \"the children of Israel\" is a term for individuals, and \"the people\" is a term for the multitude; and likewise \"and the people complained\" (Exodus 15:24). And thus our Rabbis stated (Numbers Rabbah 20:23): \"And the people began to commit harlotry\" (Numbers 25:1) — wherever \"the people\" is stated it is a term of disparagement, and wherever \"Israel\" is stated it is a term of praise.<br><br>Now, they did not say \"you have taken us to die in battle,\" but rather <b>you have taken us to die in the wilderness</b>, <b>and to die in the wilderness</b> — for before they even saw battle, they did not wish to go out into the wilderness lest they die there of famine and thirst. And it is possible that they said this to him when they departed, while they were still in the land of Egypt, when Elohim turned them by the way of the wilderness of the Sea of Suf. Or they said to him at the outset: Where shall we go? If by the way of the Philistines, they will fight against us, and if by the way of the wilderness, it is better for us to serve Egypt than to die in the wilderness. And it is further possible to say that the people believed in HaShem and prayed to Him to save them, but concerning Moses a doubt entered their hearts, lest he had taken them out in order to rule over them. And even though they saw the signs and wonders, they thought that he performed them by way of wisdom, or that HaShem brought the plagues upon them because of the wickedness of the nations — for if HaShem had desired their departure, Pharaoh would not have pursued after them.<br><br>Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 14:10) translated here <b>and they cried</b> as \"וּזְעִיקוּ\" — rendering it in the sense of complaint, meaning that they did not pray but rather complained before Him because He brought them out of Egypt, in the manner of \"and they cried to Pharaoh, saying: Why do you deal thus with your servants\" (Exodus 5:15), and similarly \"and the cry of the people and their wives was great against their brethren the Jews\" (Nehemiah 5:1) — complaining against them with a loud voice and outcry. And in the Mekhilta (here), they took hold of the craft of their fathers: <b>and they said to Moses: Because there were no graves in Egypt</b> — from the time they put leaven in the dough they came to Moses and said to him <b>Is this not the thing that we spoke to you</b>, etc. And the leaven in the dough is the evil inclination, and their intent was to say that at first they cried out to HaShem to put it in Pharaoh's heart to turn back from pursuing them, and when they saw that he was not turning back but was traveling and drawing near to them, then they said: our prayer was not accepted, and an evil thought entered their hearts to harbor suspicion against Moses, as at the beginning."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:08:01.161006351Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}